This happened because of a classic networking fallback issue related to your WSL environment!

When the DNS daemon queried the .com TLD Server, the TLD Server gave it a referral to Google's Authoritative servers (e.g. ns2.google.com). This referral included both A (IPv4) and AAAA (IPv6) IP addresses.

The daemon's logic simply scanned the list and greedily picked the first IP address it saw to continue the journey—which happened to be an AAAA (IPv6) address. However, your WSL instance doesn't have an active IPv6 route to the internet, so the socket failed with Network is unreachable. The daemon treated this as a complete failure and returned NXDOMAIN to dig.

I have just deployed a patch to resolver.c that forces the daemon to heavily prioritize finding and using A (IPv4) addresses for its internal nameserver lookups, only falling back to IPv6 if there are absolutely no IPv4 servers available.







